-- title:
--   Residually transcendental element gives a Gauss valuation subring
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, with a generator $\varpi$ of its maximal ideal, let $K_0$ be a fraction field of $R$, and let $F$ be a field equipped with compatible $R$- and $K_0$-algebra structures. Let $O$ be a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $R$ and with the image of $\varpi$ a non-unit of $O$, and let $f \in O$ satisfy the hypothesis that for every polynomial $p \in R[X]$ having at least one unit coefficient the value $p(f)$ (evaluation via $R \to F$) lies in $O$ and is a unit of $O$. The conclusion asserts first that $f \neq 0$, and then, with that fact in force: $f^{-1} \in O$; $f$ is transcendental over $R$; every element of the integral closure of $R[f]$ in $F$ and every element of the integral closure of $R[f^{-1}]$ in $F$ (the subalgebras `chartAlgFin` and `chartAlgInf`, consisting of the elements of $F$ integral over $R[f]$, resp. $R[f^{-1}]$) lies in $O$; for every $P \in R[X]$ not divisible by the constant $\varpi$, both $P(f)$ and $P(f)^{-1}$ lie in $O$; and finally, if $F$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the subfield $K_0(f)$ generated by $f$, then $O$ is a discrete valuation ring and there is a prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of the integral closure of $R[f]$ in $F$ which is a minimal prime over the ideal generated by the image of $\varpi$, is not maximal, consists exactly of those elements whose image in $F$ is a non-unit of $O$, and is such that $O$ is the corresponding localisation: $y \in O$ if and only if $y c = b$ for some $b, c$ in that chart ring with $c \notin \mathfrak p$.
--
--   This is the statement that a valuation subring in which the residue of $f$ behaves as a transcendental (every $R$-polynomial in $f$ with a unit coefficient being a unit) lies over the Gauss point of the $f$-line, and, under finiteness and separability over $K_0(f)$, is the local ring of an irreducible component of the special fibre of the two-chart integral model of that line. It feeds the construction of normal proper models from families of valuation subrings, and is used in the comparison of such families with localisations of the chart rings.
